Written from develop `362e51fe0db7e73d5557924902763fe3f10fd8c7` (`git -C "/Volumes/DevMASTER/!CODING/Secuura/Blockchain/2_Project_Files" ls-remote origin refs/heads/develop` at 05:0x on 2026-09-21, read verbs only; the tree that carries the six #1106-#1111 squashes, #1111 = KS-1223 WALLET-1 included). The test file does NOT exist at that tip: you CREATE it (`git ls-tree` of `services/referral/src/__tests__/`: 5 files — `db.retry`, `errorHandler`, `qualify.validation`, `smoke`, `validation.trim` — none by this name). The product the cells drive is `Blockchain/Dev/services/referral/src/routes/referrals.ts` (blob `e97b7f0bc550`, **345 lines**, read whole around the generate): `router.post('/generate', ...)` at `:49-89`, `const authUser = (req as any).user;` at `:53`, **`    const walletAddress = (authUser?.walletAddress as string) || (req.headers['x-wallet-address'] as string);` at `:55`** (the ONE `x-wallet-address` in the whole referral service source), the 401 for a missing `userId` at `:57-59`, `generateCodeSchema.parse(req.body)` at `:61` (every field optional, so `{}` parses), `referralService.generateCode(userId, walletAddress, body)` at `:63`, the 201 envelope at `:65-76`, `export { router as referralRoutes };` at `:345`. The harness it copies is `src/__tests__/qualify.validation.test.ts` (the KS-444 idiom: `vi.mock('../services/referralService', ...)`, `express()` + `express.json()` + `app.use('/api/referrals', referralRoutes)` on `127.0.0.1:0`, `fetch` over loopback). This service runs **VITEST** (`package.json` `"test": "vitest run"`, `vitest ^4.1.11`; the same `package.json` ALSO lists `jest ^29.7.0` and `ts-jest` in devDependencies with no jest config and no jest script — that is why the `Runner:` line above pins vitest; the builder honours it because vitest is in the service's own devDependencies).

## THE MODE — read this twice

**TEST_ONLY, NEW FILE.** Your diff touches EXACTLY ONE file: the new test file above (`--- /dev/null` / `+++ b/<path>`, the path exactly as written above, ONE hunk `@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@`). You never touch `routes/referrals.ts`, `services/referralService.ts`, `index.ts` or any other product file, and you never touch an existing test file: the behaviour is already what it is at the tip, and these cells PIN it.

## What the cells pin (one paragraph)

KS-1223 asks whether a caller-supplied `x-wallet-address` header should decide a wallet at all (the merged #1111 WALLET-1 cell pins the gateway's `trustHeaders` half; the sibling brief `KS-1223-WALLETFORWARD-1` pins the gateway's forward half). The #1106-#1111 gate then planted REFERRALFALLBACKUNPINNED — `referrals.ts:55` with the `|| (req.headers['x-wallet-address'] as string)` fallback removed — and measured **0 red of 26** across the referral suite: its five files mention no wallet at all (`grep -c -i wallet` over each: 0, 0, 0, 0, 0; positive control `grep -c -i describe`: 2, 4, 3, 2, 2). So the fallback — the value `generateCode` persists as the code's `ownerWalletAddress` when the JWT principal carries none — is unpinned in either direction. This file drives the REAL route stack over loopback exactly as the KS-444 file does (`express.json()`, a one-line stand-in for `authenticate()` that sets `req.user` to the cell's principal, the real `referralRoutes`), with `referralService.generateCode` replaced by a RECORDING `vi.fn` that answers a minimal code object. The RED cell sends `POST /api/referrals/generate` with a principal `{ userId: 'u-ks1223' }` (NO `walletAddress`) and the header `x-wallet-address: addr_client`, and asserts `[status, the walletAddress generateCode received]` equals `[201, 'addr_client']`; the CONTROL sends the same request with a principal that DOES carry `walletAddress: 'addr_user'` and asserts `[201, 'addr_user']` — the principal wins over the header at the tip AND under the tamper, so the control proves the route, the parse and the stubbed service all still run while the RED cell reds on the fallback alone. **It pins TODAY's fallback as a characterisation, not an endorsement** (the READY that shipped #1111 called this fallback "NOT pinned BY DESIGN"; the gate turned that into a row for the local model, never a ticket): if the owners decide the wallet must come from the authenticated principal only, this cell goes red on purpose and is rewritten with that decision.

## The exact change — ONE new file

Copy every line byte for byte. All 42 `+` lines are ASCII only. There is no blank line anywhere in the fence. Keep the header exactly as shown. **Your diff MUST begin with the two file-header lines, above the `@@` line: `--- /dev/null` then `+++ b/Blockchain/Dev/services/referral/src/__tests__/ks1223-wallet-header-fallback.test.ts`.**

```
@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+/**
+ * KS-1223 - the referral service's wallet FALLBACK: POST /api/referrals/generate takes the wallet from the
+ * authenticated principal and, when the principal carries none, from the caller's x-wallet-address header
+ * (routes/referrals.ts, the walletAddress line that reads authUser.walletAddress || req.headers x-wallet-address). The #1106-#1111
+ * gate planted the fallback removed and measured 0 red of 26: the suite's five files mention no wallet. The real
+ * route stack is driven over loopback (the KS-444 idiom) with referralService stubbed and RECORDING what
+ * generateCode receives. A characterisation pin of TODAY's fallback - whether a caller-supplied header should
+ * decide the wallet at all is KS-1223's question for the owners, not this file's.
+ */
+import { describe, it, expect, vi } from 'vitest';
+import express from 'express';
+const { generateCode } = vi.hoisted(() => ({
+  generateCode: vi.fn(async (userId: string, walletAddress?: string) => ({ id: 'code-ks1223', code: 'KS1223AA', createdAt: new Date('2026-09-21T00:00:00Z'), expiresAt: undefined, maxUses: undefined, customLabel: undefined, ownerId: userId, ownerWalletAddress: walletAddress })),
+}));
+vi.mock('../services/referralService', () => ({ referralService: { generateCode } }));
+/** Drive the real route stack (express.json -> a stand-in for authenticate() that sets req.user -> referralRoutes) over loopback; returns [status, the walletAddress generateCode received] */
+async function generate(user: Record<string, unknown>, headers: Record<string, string>): Promise<[number, unknown]> {
+  const { referralRoutes } = await import('../routes/referrals');
+  const app = express();
+  app.use(express.json());
+  app.use((req, _res, next) => { (req as any).user = user; next(); });
+  app.use('/api/referrals', referralRoutes);
+  const server = app.listen(0, '127.0.0.1');
+  try {
+    await new Promise<void>((r) => server.once('listening', () => r()));
+    const port = (server.address() as { port: number }).port;
+    const before = generateCode.mock.calls.length;
+    const res = await fetch('http://127.0.0.1:' + String(port) + '/api/referrals/generate', { method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', ...headers }, body: '{}' });
+    await res.text();
+    return [res.status, generateCode.mock.calls.length === before + 1 ? generateCode.mock.calls[before][1] : 'generateCode not called once'];
+  } finally {
+    server.close();
+  }
+}
+describe('KS-1223: POST /api/referrals/generate falls back to the x-wallet-address header when the principal carries no wallet', () => {
+  it('RED KS-1223: a principal without walletAddress plus x-wallet-address: addr_client answers 201 and generateCode receives addr_client', async () => {
+    expect(await generate({ userId: 'u-ks1223' }, { 'x-wallet-address': 'addr_client' })).toEqual([201, 'addr_client']);
+  });
+  it('CONTROL: a principal WITH walletAddress wins over the header - generateCode receives addr_user', async () => {
+    expect(await generate({ userId: 'u-ks1223', walletAddress: 'addr_user' }, { 'x-wallet-address': 'addr_client' })).toEqual([201, 'addr_user']);
+  });
+});
```

`vi.mock('../services/referralService', ...)` is hoisted by vitest above the imports, and `vi.hoisted` lifts the `generateCode` spy with it, so `routes/referrals.ts`'s own `import { referralService } from '../services/referralService'` resolves to the recording stub — no pool, no `../db`, no Redis (the KS-444 file mocks the same module the same way). `generate(user, headers)` mirrors the KS-444 `postQualify`: `express()`, `express.json()`, ONE extra middleware that sets `(req as any).user = user` (the stand-in for `@secuura/shared`'s `authenticate()` that `index.ts:81` mounts in front of the routes), then `app.use('/api/referrals', referralRoutes)`; it listens on `127.0.0.1:0` (waiting for `'listening'` before reading the port, the KS-845 note), POSTs `{}` with the cell's headers, and returns `[status, generateCode.mock.calls[before][1]]` — the SECOND argument of the one call the request made (`generateCode(userId, walletAddress, body)`, `:63`), or the string `'generateCode not called once'` if the call count did not rise by exactly one. Nothing connects to a fixed port; the cells need no database, no JWT, no env and no product bytes.

